Summary &mdash; From the Translator

This teaching develops the parallel between the role of the judge and the dynamics of prayer, both grounded in the concept of dibbur emes &mdash; speaking truth. Just as one who prays must speak truth so that G-d&rsquo;s light illuminates and reveals the way through the darkness of confusion, so too the judge must ensure that every word of his ruling is spoken in truth, without any inclination &mdash; and thereby the light of Hashem (which is truth) enters the courtroom and reveals who is actually telling the truth.

The central Zoharic formula &mdash; &ldquo;Tzedek, when joined with emes, becomes emunah&rdquo; &mdash; is applied throughout: the judge&rsquo;s truthful speech (emes) rectifies the fallen faith (emunah/tzedek) that was blemished by the dishonest litigant. This is why judgment occurs during the day (the aspect of truth), why the judge is called &ldquo;Elokim&rdquo; (G-d&rsquo;s light is clothed within him), why true judges must be ordained in Eretz Yisroel (the land = emunah), and why even the smallest monetary unit &mdash; the p&rsquo;rutah &mdash; is significant enough to warrant judicial attention, since it alludes to the whispered prayer of the lips (the gateway of emunah).

&mdash; Translator&rsquo;s summary for ajew.org

Conceptual Flow

&#9654;

Core principle from Torah 9: When praying, speak dibbur emes (a word of truth) &rarr; this is the light of Hashem Himself &rarr; it illuminates the openings through the darkness

&#8595;

&#9654;

Two litigants come to court: one claims falsely &rarr; this is a blemish of emunah (he did not deal faithfully) &rarr; falsehood conceals truth

&#8595;

&#9654;

The judge&rsquo;s remedy: speak only truth, with no inclination &rarr; &ldquo;G-d stands in the congregation of the mighty&rdquo; &rarr; truth (= G-d&rsquo;s light) illuminates who is right &rarr; din emes la&rsquo;amiso

&#8595;

&#9654;

Three judges = three lines of emes (aleph-mem-sav = beginning-middle-end) = three Patriarchs = Yaakov = truth & judgment &rarr; sustain the three worlds

&#8595;

&#9654;

Bribery blinds &mdash; because without truth there is no light, and without light one cannot see

&#8595;

&#9654;

Judgment by day, not by night: day = emes; night = emunah in smallness &rarr; tzedek + emes = emunah &rarr; evening + morning = &ldquo;one day&rdquo;

&#8595;

&#9654;

The judge is called &ldquo;Elokim&rdquo; &mdash; G-d&rsquo;s light clothes itself in the truthful judge

&#8595;

&#9654;

Judges in Eretz Yisroel = the Land is emunah; P&rsquo;rutah = emunah/t&rsquo;fillah (uf&rsquo;ritah) &rarr; even the smallest monetary blemish requires judicial rectification
